Option (b), with one precision: the dispatcher routes to that platform's collection step, which captures raw textual output, and the value printed to stdout on success is the parsed list of device records produced by the same generic parsing contract — the platform only determines where the raw text comes from, never the parsing behavior. It does not return the name of an inspection routine. The full concrete input/output contract is pinned in the public test cases; here they are for feature2_1_platform_enumeration, verbatim:

{
  "description": "Enumerating devices for a recognized operating-system platform routes to that platform's collection step, captures its raw textual output, and returns the parsed list of device records. The same parsing contract applies regardless of platform; only the supplied raw output differs.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "action": "list",
        "platform": "linux",
        "output": "device: \"/dev/sda\"\ndescription: \"My drive\"\nsize: \"15 GB\"\nmountpoint: \"/mnt/drive\""
      },
      "expected_output": "[{\"device\":\"/dev/sda\",\"description\":\"My drive\",\"size\":\"15 GB\",\"mountpoint\":\"/mnt/drive\"}]\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "action": "list",
        "platform": "win32",
        "output": "device: \"\\\\\\\\.\\\\PHYSICALDRIVE1\"\ndescription: \"My drive\"\nsize: \"15 GB\"\nmountpoint: \"D:\""
      },
      "expected_output": "[{\"device\":\"\\\\\\\\.\\\\PHYSICALDRIVE1\",\"description\":\"My drive\",\"size\":\"15 GB\",\"mountpoint\":\"D:\"}]\n"
    }
  ]
}

Note the win32 case: the raw output wraps values in double-quotes, and the stored values carry the quote-stripped literal content. That is the whole contract — the same parsing contract applies regardless of platform; only the supplied raw output differs.

